1
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:08,685
It's the year one hundred and thirty. The edge of the Roman world is a wall.

2
00:00:08,735 --> 00:00:14,865
Seventy-three miles of stone, from one sea to the other, across the neck of Britain.

3
00:00:14,915 --> 00:00:19,335
And tonight, in the fog, you're standing on top of it.

4
00:00:19,435 --> 00:00:26,125
Somewhere out there, four men are missing. By the end of this day, you'll know why.

5
00:00:31,225 --> 00:00:33,085
Twenty hours earlier.

6
00:00:33,135 --> 00:00:36,605
You wake up before the trumpet. You always do.

7
00:00:36,655 --> 00:00:43,440
Your name is Gaius. You're twenty-six. You've been a soldier of the Sixth Legion for eight years.

8
00:00:43,490 --> 00:00:46,255
And you share this room with seven other men.

9
00:00:46,305 --> 00:00:52,110
It's about the size of a small bedroom. Two rows of bunks, one table, one oil lamp.

10
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:55,250
You know every one of them by the way they snore.

11
00:00:55,300 --> 00:01:00,410
Your shield hangs on the wall. So does your helmet, your cloak, and everything you own.

12
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:04,340
Then the trumpet sounds, and eight men get dressed in the dark.

13
00:01:04,390 --> 00:01:11,100
Tunic. Belt. Sword on the right hip, dagger on the left. Boots with iron nails in the soles.

14
00:01:11,150 --> 00:01:17,890
The armour goes on last. Plates of iron over the shoulders and chest. It takes two men to buckle it.

15
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:22,100
Breakfast is porridge. Wheat, water, a little salt.

16
00:01:22,150 --> 00:01:25,620
It was breakfast yesterday. It will be breakfast tomorrow.

17
00:01:25,670 --> 00:01:33,330
There's bread, if someone baked. Cheese, if someone traded for it. And sour wine mixed with water, called posca.

18
00:01:33,380 --> 00:01:38,870
It tastes like vinegar. You drink it anyway. The water up here can make you sick.

19
00:01:39,070 --> 00:01:48,500
And every bite is taken out of your pay. Food, boots, bedding. Rome pays you three hundred silver coins a year, and then sends you the bill.

20
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:56,935
The yard of the milecastle. First light.

21
00:01:56,985 --> 00:02:05,655
A milecastle is a small fort built into the Wall itself, one every Roman mile. Between each pair, two stone towers.

22
00:02:05,705 --> 00:02:10,345
Your century lines up in the mud. Shields grounded. Spears upright.

23
00:02:10,395 --> 00:02:18,015
The centurion walks the line. You can tell him by the crest on his helmet, running side to side, and the vine stick in his hand.

24
00:02:18,065 --> 00:02:22,145
He uses the stick on men who are late. Nobody is late.

25
00:02:22,195 --> 00:02:26,625
His deputy, the optio, reads the roster from a wooden tablet.

26
00:02:26,675 --> 00:02:31,855
Three men sick. Two on leave. One in the guardhouse for selling army boots.

27
00:02:31,905 --> 00:02:38,795
And four scouts, still out past the Wall. They left two days ago. They should have been back last night.

28
00:02:38,845 --> 00:02:43,560
Nobody says anything. The centurion just reads out today's job.

29
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:49,082
The frost has cracked the Wall. Again. And your century is going to fix it.

30
00:02:54,182 --> 00:02:58,237
The Wall runs along the top of the crags, like a spine.

31
00:02:58,287 --> 00:03:05,502
On the north side, the ground drops away in cliffs. Below them, a lake, grey as the sky.

32
00:03:05,552 --> 00:03:13,372
On this side, the south, sheep. Smoke from a farm. And the road home, a long way down.

33
00:03:13,422 --> 00:03:20,422
The Wall is about fifteen feet high and ten feet thick. There's a walkway on top, behind a parapet.

34
00:03:20,472 --> 00:03:25,112
It was built eight years ago, by the legions. By men like you.

35
00:03:25,162 --> 00:03:31,582
Emperor Hadrian came here himself, they say. He looked at the hills and said: build it here.

36
00:03:31,632 --> 00:03:34,607
And now it's your job to keep it standing.

37
00:03:34,657 --> 00:03:44,472
Winter is the enemy. Rain soaks into the stone. At night it freezes, swells, and pushes the face stones out.

38
00:03:44,522 --> 00:03:52,882
Here, a whole section has burst. You can see the core: rubble and mortar, packed in like gravel in a riverbed.

39
00:03:52,932 --> 00:04:01,077
You carry the new stones up from the pile. Each one is a block of sandstone, cut square, as heavy as a child.

40
00:04:01,127 --> 00:04:07,492
Up the ramp. Into the gap. Mortar. Tap it level with a mallet. Next one.

41
00:04:07,542 --> 00:04:13,732
By mid-morning your shoulders are burning, and it's started to rain. Of course it has.

42
00:04:13,782 --> 00:04:19,937
Then, set into the Wall beside the gap, you see it. A stone with letters on it.

43
00:04:19,987 --> 00:04:24,587
First cohort. The century of Julius Priscus.

44
00:04:24,637 --> 00:04:32,457
Every stretch of this Wall is signed by the men who built it. This stone was carved by your century, eight years ago.

45
00:04:33,057 --> 00:04:40,697
Priscus is dead now. But his name is still holding up the Wall. And you're still holding up his.

46
00:04:45,797 --> 00:04:50,557
Noon. You eat sitting in the lee of the Wall, out of the wind.

47
00:04:50,607 --> 00:04:55,752
Bread. A slice of bacon. And a cup of beer, brewed by the locals.

48
00:04:55,802 --> 00:05:03,812
Beer matters here. One officer near this Wall wrote to his commander: my comrades have no beer. Please send some.

49
00:05:03,862 --> 00:05:09,752
We know, because his letter survived. It was found in the mud, two thousand years later.

50
00:05:09,802 --> 00:05:15,622
So you write a letter too. Not on paper. On a thin slice of wood, in ink.

51
00:05:15,672 --> 00:05:21,247
You tell your mother you're well. You tell her the food is fine. Both are lies.

52
00:05:21,297 --> 00:05:23,547
Then you ask her to send socks.

53
00:05:23,597 --> 00:05:27,197
Two pairs of sandals. And two pairs of underpants.

54
00:05:27,247 --> 00:05:35,152
That's not a joke. A real soldier on this frontier asked his family for exactly that. His letter survived too.

55
00:05:35,202 --> 00:05:40,242
Because the real enemy up here isn't the tribes. It's the cold.

56
00:05:40,292 --> 00:05:45,412
After lunch, the bathhouse. Even out here, Romans need a hot bath.

57
00:05:45,462 --> 00:05:53,797
Warm room. Hot room. A scrape with a curved blade instead of soap. Then a cold plunge that stops your heart.

58
00:05:53,847 --> 00:06:01,194
Then the latrine. Twenty men sit in a row, side by side, and share one sponge on a stick.

59
00:06:01,594 --> 00:06:04,074
Nobody talks about the sponge.

60
00:06:09,174 --> 00:06:12,204
In the afternoon, you're on gate duty.

61
00:06:12,254 --> 00:06:18,624
The Wall isn't a wall to keep people out. It's a door. And Rome decides who comes through it.

62
00:06:18,674 --> 00:06:24,014
Farmers bring cattle south to sell. Traders bring wool, hides, and amber.

63
00:06:24,064 --> 00:06:28,674
Every one of them pays a toll. Every bag gets opened.

64
00:06:28,724 --> 00:06:34,144
In the official reports, the Romans call the locals Brittunculi. Little Brits.

65
00:06:34,194 --> 00:06:40,674
You've been here long enough to know they're not little. And they know these hills better than you ever will.

66
00:06:40,724 --> 00:06:49,144
A trader with red hair and a pack of hides waits in line. When you search his bag, he speaks quietly, in bad Latin.

67
00:06:49,194 --> 00:06:56,284
He says there's been smoke in the north. Fires on the hills, three nights running. Signal fires.

68
00:06:56,334 --> 00:06:58,654
He says the tribes are moving.

69
00:06:59,154 --> 00:07:05,079
Then he takes his pack, pays his toll, and walks south without looking back.

70
00:07:10,179 --> 00:07:15,544
An hour later, the centurion picks five men to go out and look for the scouts.

71
00:07:15,594 --> 00:07:17,284
You're one of them.

72
00:07:17,334 --> 00:07:23,654
You go out through the north gate. Across the ditch, which is nine feet deep and full of rainwater.

73
00:07:23,704 --> 00:07:25,754
And then there's nothing.

74
00:07:25,804 --> 00:07:34,004
No road. No farms. No Wall. Just moor, and heather, and bog, rolling north for ever.

75
00:07:34,054 --> 00:07:40,224
The sun is low and gold. Your shadows stretch out behind you, back toward Rome.

76
00:07:40,274 --> 00:07:43,324
Two miles out, you find their camp.

77
00:07:43,374 --> 00:07:52,824
A ring of stones. A fire that went cold days ago. A shield lying face down in the heather. A spear, snapped in half.

78
00:07:53,124 --> 00:07:56,524
There are no bodies. There's no blood.

79
00:07:57,024 --> 00:08:02,604
There's just one thing, laid neatly on a flat rock, like a message.

80
00:08:03,204 --> 00:08:06,991
A Roman helmet. Filled with water from the bog.

81
00:08:07,391 --> 00:08:12,231
Nobody touches it. You walk back to the Wall faster than you came.

82
00:08:17,331 --> 00:08:22,391
At dusk, the Wall turns pink, then purple, then black.

83
00:08:22,441 --> 00:08:30,921
On the turret, the signal fire is lit. If anything happens tonight, the next tower will see it, and the next, and the next.

84
00:08:30,971 --> 00:08:36,241
A message can cross this whole country in an hour. Faster than any horse.

85
00:08:36,291 --> 00:08:43,141
The centurion writes his report. Four scouts missing. Camp abandoned. Smoke reported in the north.

86
00:08:43,191 --> 00:08:48,811
Then he gives out tonight's password, written on a wooden tag, passed from man to man.

87
00:08:48,861 --> 00:08:51,516
Tonight's word is victory.

88
00:08:51,816 --> 00:08:54,326
You don't say it like you believe it.

89
00:08:54,376 --> 00:09:02,361
You eat. You don't taste it. Then you clean your armour, because a centurion will check it whether the world is ending or not.

90
00:09:07,461 --> 00:09:13,486
The night is cut into four watches. You have the third. After midnight, before dawn.

91
00:09:13,536 --> 00:09:18,396
You climb up to the walkway, and take the spear from the man you're replacing.

92
00:09:18,446 --> 00:09:24,066
He gives you the password. You give it back. Then he's gone, and you're alone.

93
00:09:24,116 --> 00:09:32,651
Fog has come up from the lake. It's so thick you can't see the next tower. Only its fire, a smudge of orange in the grey.

94
00:09:32,701 --> 00:09:40,146
To the south, everything you know. Your bunk, your seven snoring friends, your bread and your beer.

95
00:09:40,446 --> 00:09:44,891
To the north, nothing. The end of the world.

96
00:09:44,941 --> 00:09:48,676
You stand there for an hour. Listening.

97
00:09:48,726 --> 00:09:55,406
A sheep coughs somewhere down the slope. Water drips off your helmet. The brazier ticks as it cools.

98
00:09:55,806 --> 00:09:57,606
Then you see it.

99
00:09:58,076 --> 00:10:02,476
Out on the moor, in the fog, there's a light.

100
00:10:02,526 --> 00:10:07,786
One small orange light, where there's nothing for twenty miles.

101
00:10:07,836 --> 00:10:10,336
You watch it for a long time.

102
00:10:10,736 --> 00:10:14,156
And you realise it isn't standing still.

103
00:10:14,356 --> 00:10:16,696
It's moving toward the Wall.

104
00:10:16,746 --> 00:10:20,266
Then a second light appears beside it. Then a third.

105
00:10:20,316 --> 00:10:23,496
You reach for the horn on the wall beside you.

106
00:10:23,796 --> 00:10:32,826
Seventy-three miles of stone. Fifteen thousand men. And right now, the whole Roman Empire ends with you.

107
00:10:33,026 --> 00:10:35,436
You lift the horn to your lips.

108
00:10:35,936 --> 00:10:37,736
And you blow.
